import AxQM.Concrete.StabilizerIndependence

/-!
# Nielsen & Chuang, Proposition 10.3 — independent generators ↔ linearly independent check rows

*(N&C §10.5.1, p. 457.)*

For S=<g1..gl>, -I not in S: generators independent iff check-matrix rows linearly independent.

## Context

A set of stabilizer generators is **independent** (N&C eq. 10.82) when dropping any one of them
strictly shrinks the group generated, `⟨g₁, …, ĝᵢ, …, g_l⟩ ≠ ⟨g₁, …, g_l⟩`. Deciding independence
directly is "rather time-consuming"; Proposition 10.3 reduces it to a linear-algebra check on the
**check matrix**, the `l × 2n` matrix over `𝔽₂ = ZMod 2` whose `i`-th row is the check row
`r(gᵢ) = (x | z)` recording where `gᵢ` has `X`/`Y` factors (left block) and `Z`/`Y` factors (right
block). N&C's proof turns on two facts: the row map is additive, `r(g g') = r(g) + r(g')` ("addition
in the row representation corresponds to multiplication of group elements"), and `-I ∉ S` forces the
row map to have trivial kernel on `S` (`r(g) = 0`, `g ∈ S ⟹ g = I`, via Exercises 10.30/10.35). A
linear dependence `∑ aᵢ r(gᵢ) = 0` with some `aⱼ ≠ 0` is then equivalent to `∏ gᵢ^{aᵢ} = I`, i.e.
to `gⱼ` being a product of the other generators — precisely a failure of independence. This
"innocuous looking" proposition is used repeatedly in the rest of the chapter (it is the opening
step of Propositions 10.4 and 10.5).

## Content
 The mathematics is the abstract `n`-qubit Pauli group `Concrete.PauliGroup n`, its subgroups, the
check-row homomorphism `PauliGroup.checkRowHom` (valued in `ZMod 2`), and a group ↔
`𝔽₂`-linear-independence bridge. Compare Exercise 10.33
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliStringCommutation.lean`), the check-row infrastructure
(`AxQM/Concrete/CheckMatrix.lean`), and Proposition 10.4
(`AxQM/Concrete/StabilizerGeneratorFlip.lean`).

## Faithfulness

The theorem is stated exactly as N&C states it: `IndependentGenerators g` is N&C's eq. (10.82)
verbatim (`⟨rest⟩ ≠ ⟨all⟩` for every dropped generator), the hypothesis is `-I ∉ S` (`PauliGroup.
negOne n ∉ Subgroup.closure (Set.range g)`), and "check-matrix rows linearly independent" is
`LinearIndependent (ZMod 2)` of the family of check rows `PauliGroup.checkRowHom ∘ g`. No hypothesis
is added or strengthened. The proof follows N&C's contrapositive: the two facts
it uses (additivity of the row map; trivial kernel from `-I ∉ S`) are Pauli-group
results, and the equivalence itself is the reusable abstract lemma
`independent_iff_linearIndependent_of_hom` (which also shows the hypotheses `-I ∉ S` supplies are
*minimal* — trivial kernel into an `𝔽₂`-module already makes `S` elementary abelian, so no separate
`gᵢ² = I` assumption is needed).

## Formalisation

The formalisation is in `AxQM/Concrete/StabilizerIndependence.lean` (namespace `AxQM.Concrete`).
It uses `AxQM/Concrete/CheckMatrix.lean`, `AxQM/Concrete/PauliGroup.lean` and
`AxQM/Concrete/PauliGroupNegOne.lean`.

* `stabilizerGenerators_independent_iff_checkRow_linearIndependent` — **Proposition 10.3 itself**:
  for `g : Fin l → PauliGroup n` with `-I ∉ S = ⟨range g⟩`, `IndependentGenerators g ↔
  LinearIndependent (ZMod 2) (fun i => PauliGroup.checkRowHom (g i))`.
* `IndependentGenerators` — N&C's independence predicate (eq. 10.82) for a family of group
  generators: dropping any one strictly shrinks the subgroup generated.
* `independentGenerators_iff_forall_notMem` — independence is equivalent to "no generator lies in
  the subgroup generated by the others".
* `independent_iff_linearIndependent_of_hom` — the reusable **abstract core**: for a group `G`, an
  additive `φ : G → V` into an `𝔽₂`-module with trivial kernel on `S = ⟨range g⟩`, the generators
  are independent iff `φ ∘ g` is `𝔽₂`-linearly independent (used for Propositions 10.4 and 10.5).

See the module docstring of `AxQM/Concrete/StabilizerIndependence.lean` for the full rationale.
-/
